  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with the water scene, and it passes typecheck. I couldn't run it in a browser or run `vite build`, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. The shader compile status and whether the colours look right are unchecked.\n\n- **Water:** a custom GLSL material on a 321×321 grid, about 103k vertices, spaced densely near the camera and sparsely out to 300 m. A vertex shader sums five sine waves with an analytic normal. Short waves fade out with distance.\n- **Fragment shading:** a fresnel term mixes the water body colour with a reflection of the sky. The sky has noise-based clouds, and noise ripples (fbm) perturb the normal. A sun specular glint and whitecaps on tall crests are added. Haze blends the water into the horizon colour.\n- **Sky:** a large sphere with its own gradient shader, drawn double-sided with no depth write. It and the water reflection call the same sky function, so they match.\n- **Animation and camera:** a `waveClock` uniform is updated every `requestAnimationFrame`. The camera is at height 3.2, looking slightly down toward the horizon, with the sun low ahead and to the left.\n- **Colour output:** the app sets `output.toneMapping` to `\"none\"`. Both shaders write linear light, apply an ACES-style curve themselves, and decode their sRGB colours with `a3dSrgbToLinear`. The output stage then only sRGB-encodes, as `.agents/skills/aura3d-materials-environments/references/custom-shader-chunks.md` describes. This avoids the double tone-mapping that would wash out the colours.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and the third frame of my `requestAnimationFrame` loop.\n\nI didn't override the pixel ratio or resize the canvas.\n\n",
